<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tours</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Caesarodunum, Civitas Turonorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tours is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> in west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the historic capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touraine">Touraine</a>. Founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> as Caesarodunum, the chief town of the Turones, it became a great pilgrimage centre after the death of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-of-tours">Saint Martin</a> in 397. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a> wrote his histories there, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin</a> led its abbey school under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, and in 732 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> defeated an Umayyad army between Tours and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tours.jpg" alt="Tours" width="320" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jules78120 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tram_et_h%C3%B4tel_de_ville_de_Tours.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
